A||||9||Joe Torma||Clutch Master--Rebuild or Get New?||||5||
Z||000000||Joe Torma||10-17-2001||02:07 PM||||Anyone rebuild their clutch master cyl with the Fierostore kit? Or should I just get a new one?<P>I'm kinda broke so $50 extra for a new one is a lot right now.<P>TIA<BR>Joe||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||10-17-2001||03:02 PM||||The success rate for rebuilding master cylinders or slave cylinders is about 50%, so I would buy a new one.  If you spend the $20 or so for the rebuild kit and it fails you just added $20 to the cost of a new one, so you would be better off not going that route.<P>Don't buy any new master cylinder.....only buy a genuine GM one.  They are better than the parts stores ones (most of which are rebuilt) and you are almost guaranteed that it will be good out of the box. The same can't be said for the aftermarket ones.  BTW.....this goes for the slave cylinder too.<P>Good luck.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||10-17-2001||03:26 PM||||Good point...I ordered new.  I did rebuild my slave and it seems to be ok.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||SLYGUY||10-17-2001||07:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B>The success rate for rebuilding master cylinders or slave cylinders is about 50%, so I would buy a new one.  If you spend the $20 or so for the rebuild kit and it fails you just added $20 to the cost of a new one, so you would be better off not going that route.<P>Don't buy any new master cylinder.....only buy a genuine GM one.  They are better than the parts stores ones (most of which are rebuilt) and you are almost guaranteed that it will be good out of the box. The same can't be said for the aftermarket ones.  BTW.....this goes for the slave cylinder too.<P>Good luck.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I will only buy a new one knowing what I do now. Rebuilt ones are a tremendous waste of both time and energy. <BR>Check my post: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014482.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014482.html</A> <P>Sly<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||onfire||10-18-2001||03:14 AM||||I must be a lucky person then. When I had my v6 4speed Fiero I rebuilt both the master cylinder and slave cylinder. After I replaced the clutch, which wasn't the problem. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> I did this in the apt. I live in. 1 day to take it out, 1 day to put it back in. (the clutch). After I rebuilt the master and slave cylinders, everything worked great. I sold the car a year later.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/onfiresigtoo.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||TooManyFieros||10-18-2001||02:37 PM||||I think rebuilding is OK if you know what is beyond repair and what is not. Also, you need to have a hone and know how to use it. Guess it's like rebuilding the engine. If you don't know, either learn or get it done. Hey Joe, ready for some Air Conditioning conversion work? <BR>       Jim Gilbert||||reg||1
Z||000006||Joe Torma||10-18-2001||07:49 PM||||Hehe...yeah, actually, I guess could fill my AC now.  But I have to get the filter dohicky first.  The system has held vacuum for 1 month now.  Hmmm...I's gots a question...time for a new thread.<P>Hopefully the clutch master cyl is it.  I have a new certerforce in there.||||reg||1
Z||000007||recluse||10-18-2001||07:55 PM||||After past experiences, from now on I buy ONLY GM hydraulic parts, and am not even going to bother rebuilding anymore.  Put a Fiero store slave on and it went bad a few months later.  Put GM slave and master on and its never felt better.  The clutch even seems to grab a little better.||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||10-18-2001||10:03 PM||||I agree I have used aftermarket slave cylinders and master cylinders and they either don't work right or fail within a few months. Bought I GM unit and have had no problems since.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||SLYGUY||10-18-2001||11:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B>I agree I have used aftermarket slave cylinders and master cylinders and they either don't work right or fail within a few months. Bought I GM unit and have had no problems since.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As you may or may not have already read...I bought a rebuilt and it was total junk! Bought a new GM for $30 more and it works great. <BR>Took about 1-2 hours to bleed with a mightyvac, would of taken much longer with an assistant inside the car, clutch works great. It feels much better now<P><BR>Sly<P>||||reg||1
